# Chapter 28 — Exception

Of his four hands, the fourth was the one Seln used least and trusted most. The card on his door said Shadow, Bronze-tier, and the card was true; the hands were the part of him no card recorded.

The round clerk's script belonged to the copying table, and everybody in the wing had seen it. The quick cramped one was for his own notes, and nobody had. The third, a merchant's sloping hand with a flourish on its capitals, was for wrappers addressed to a cloth house up the coast that had never sold a yard of cloth. The fourth was upright and plain and a little old-fashioned, the hand of a man who had been taught to write by somebody strict, and it was for the product alone. He took it out that night a little after the ninth bell, in his room on the staff corridor, with the shutter fastened and the lamp turned down to a bead.

On the table in front of him lay what the afternoon had left. The warden's draft, which he had copied himself at the wing's long table not two hours since, in the round hand, every word of it, because the wing copied everything. The duty clerk's entry, four lines long: *Hall three, north bay, sixth bell. Apparatus failure, resistance post. No evaluable content. Warden attending.* And the thing that was not on the table, because it was in him: eleven paces of open boards, and what had happened at the far end of them.

A clean sheet. The heading, *Exception report: floor incident*, because any afternoon outside the routine went up under that heading, whatever it was found to contain. The date. His brief's number. Then, before he wrote a word of the body, he did what he always did before a report that mattered, and called up the auditor.

The auditor was himself, at twenty-two. Two years of his life he had spent at a long table in a records house up the coast, with a red pencil and a stack of other officers' product, reading for the places where it lied. He had been very good at it. He had been, he thought now, rather a cold young man, and quite right about most things, and he kept that young man in the back of his head the way some people keep a dead teacher, to be consulted. So he sat the young man down at the table, in his mind, and gave him the boy's baseline to hold in his left hand, and waited for the report to be put into his right.

He knew what the report had to carry. He had known it since the forearm came up.

He had stood with the dial sheets against his chest and watched a post lock at the seventh setting and send the third-year's whole delivery back off the pad. He had watched a boy who had been six feet away arrive between the third-year and the pad in less time than the delivery took to come back. Seln's whole craft was being where nobody looked for him.

In fifteen years he had never seen anybody get anywhere that fast, at that size, in that direction, toward the danger and not away. And then the boy's arm had taken the return the way a stone quay takes a barge coming in too fast: it had not given, and it had not broken, and the force had gone on down through the boy into the boards. Two things, at the least, and the file's classification had a name for neither.

His brief had categories, as every brief did. The second of them was the one the whole posting existed to fill, if it ever needed filling: *observed capability materially in excess of the subject's documented baseline.* Seln had filed under it eleven times in fifteen years. Eleven times he had been right. Eleven people had gone, afterward, into files further up the coast that he had never been shown and had never asked to see.

He dipped his pen and wrote.

*Hall three (east), north bay, sixth bell, Third-day. Apparatus failure during a supervised floor hour: the retaining pin of a resistance post sheared under load at the seventh setting, locking the core during a Force Path third-year's fourth declaration of his third set and returning the delivery off the pad. Subject was at the bay's edge on an adjoining footwork drill, signed for by the Ash Path instructor, and intervened. The third-year was not struck. No injury requiring the infirmary. The academy's floor warden attended within the quarter-hour, withdrew all four posts on the bluff from use, and has recommended inspection and replacement of every retaining pin in the academy's apparatus; her draft and the wing's log are enclosed. Response consistent with documented baseline.*

He blotted it, and handed it across the table to the young man with the red pencil.

---

The young man read it the way he had read everything, with the baseline in his left hand, looking first for a quarrel.

That was always the first thing: whether two papers about the same man disagreed, and Seln watched him look.

Gault's office had filed the baseline in the boy's first weeks, and it had been copied into the product soon after. It gave a practitioner of non-standard architecture who executed consistently. It gave a displacement framework, measured to a working ceiling on sprung timber. And it gave a plate trial at the frame's heaviest weight, in which the enrollee had taken the blow on a braced arm and kept his feet.

So the young man found, on record, a boy who could move suddenly and could take a blow on his arm. In the report he found a boy who had moved suddenly and taken a blow on his arm, and a closing sentence saying the two agreed. Nothing quarrelled, and the red pencil did not move.

Next he weighed it. He had a habit, Seln remembered, of lifting a report in his palm before he read it closely, as though he could feel its heft through the paper. He had learned in his first month that frightened officers wrote thin. A man with something to hide wanted to give the machine as little as he could, and so on the days that most needed a full account he sent up three lines and a signature. Seln had caught two men that way himself, at that long table, by nothing more than the thinness of what they sent on a busy day.

So this report was not thin. Apparatus, cause, setting, the drill, the instructor's signature, the outcome, the warden, the posts, the pins, the enclosures. The young man hefted it in his imagined hand and set it down satisfied. It weighed what a day like that ought to weigh.

Last, and slowest, he read for warmth.

That was the hardest test, and the one Seln had been proudest of passing other men's product through, long ago. A man's attention could not be hidden, because it was not a thing he chose; it pooled of its own accord wherever he was interested, and the sentences there grew longer and fuller and a little warm, like a hearthstone. Find where a report grew warm and you knew where its writer's mind had been. Find a cold place where warmth should have been, and you had found the hole.

This report was warm about the pin.

It lingered on the shear and the setting, the core and the collar, the warden coming within the quarter-hour, every post on the bluff withdrawn, every pin in the academy to be inspected and replaced. It was warm about the institution, and the young man would have nodded at that. Naturally the writer cared about the institution. It was what a man in an assessment wing was best placed to see. And an academy finding a flaw in nine years of its own apparatus was exactly the weather the analysts liked best.

And it was true. That was the whole of the art, and the young man with the red pencil had never understood it, because he had only ever been on the reading side of the table. A gap in a report did not survive because it was well hidden. It survived because every word around it was real.

Seln was interested in the pin. On the walk back from the hall it had filled his head. A tired pin in a nine-year collar, found in a term when an inspection might come at any time, with the posts chained and the warden's draft already on its way to faculty, was the best weather he had recorded since the posting began.

The young man finished, and put the report down, and found nothing. He had been very good, and he found nothing, because there was nothing on the page to find.

Seln sat looking at it after the young man had gone.

The boy's crossing was not in it. The boy's arm was not in it, except as *intervened*. No sentence formed about it. He saw it the way a man coming into his own room in the dark knows, without lighting a lamp, that one chair has been moved a hand's breadth from where it stood. The room was a different shape, and he stood in the shape for a while and looked at it.

He found it would bear weight.

He folded the sheet, and sealed it, and wrote the wrapper in the merchant's sloping hand.

---

He walked it down, as he always did. The academy's own bag went to Ostrand twice a day for nothing, and that was precisely why nothing of his ever went in it.

Out by the wing's side door, with his own key. The dark length of the north quadrangle. A nod to the night porter at the lodge, returned without the porter's eyes leaving his newspaper. Then the four long bends of the bluff road, down toward the lamps of the river.

At the foot of the last bend, by the toll board, two grey coats stamped under their lamp. He let the Path run on that stretch from long habit and felt their attention lift toward him and touch him and slide off like water off oilcloth. Neither of them would ever know that the one paper in a month worth carrying had gone by them in a satchel at arm's length.

Halfway along the river street it began to rain. It was a fine cold rain that did not so much fall as gather on him, on his shoulders and the brim of his hat and the backs of his hands. It was still gathering when he paid the courier's clerk by the fish steps from his own purse and watched the packet go into the canvas sack. It was still gathering an hour and ten minutes after he had left, on the last bend going up. He had made that walk five times this term and never once minded it, and he did not mind it now.

It was past the eleventh bell when the duty key turned in the wing's side door. There was a week's ledger of floor hours waiting, to be added up and set against the instructors' sheets before morning.

He added it up, four long columns, and got it right.

He got such sums right four times in five, and took care never to be right oftener, because a man who never slipped at figures was a man people eventually began to watch. Last week, in front of the desk clerk, he had carried a ten into the wrong column and been put right very kindly, and had thanked her, and coloured a little. Tonight there was nobody in front of whom to be wrong, so he was not wrong.

Then he pulled out the cabinet drawer where the term's carbons were kept. It held a carbon of everything that had gone down the coast since the term began: calendar extracts, floor issues, the list of the boy's associates, the first product with its line about a private notation habit, and each month after it. The drawer ran out on its runners without a whisper, because he had waxed them his first week.

The whole wing was silent. Far below the bluff, the river was going about its business.

He filed the report. Then he sat for a while with the file drawer open, not filing anything else.

---

Cael told Lira and Brom that night in Brom's room with the door shut. He sat on the end of the bed; Brom sat on the floor against the wall with his knees up; Lira stood at the window.

He told them all of it, plainly, in order: the grind that did not come, the read on time and the wrong shape, the Wind sent forward, Reydan's fragment on contact, the floor giving a third of it back, the landing beat in front of the jammed post. Then Jask, and the true short answer. Then the drawing, and the nine dots, and the ninth.

Nobody said anything for a while when he had finished.

"Your arm," said Lira at last, with her back still to the room.

"It hums."

"Hums."

"Like a bell after somebody's stopped ringing it. It hasn't stopped." He moved the fingers of his left hand to show her they moved. "It's not the ordinary ache. I don't know what it is yet. I've written it down."

Lira turned round then and looked at the arm, and then at him, for a long time, and he watched her decide not to say any of the first four things she wanted to say.

"Jask's walking about," she said instead.

"Jask's walking about."

"Good." She sat down on the window ledge with her bad leg out. "Then it was right. Whatever comes next."

Brom had his chin on his knees. "What comes next?"

"Something goes up the coast. It has to." Cael told them the rest of the page: another officer, perhaps; more coats at the road; a letter to Withrow. "It isn't him being wicked. It's what he was sent for. Lira, I don't think it's your terms. He hasn't touched my paper wrong. He'll write it down right."

"I know what my terms were," said Lira. "I said *wrong*. I'm not a child." Her eyes went past the glass to the dark where the ferry landing lay. "That's what makes it worse. Every time this man has a chance to be easy to hate, he does his work properly instead."

"So we wait," said Brom.

"We get ready," said Cael. "And then we wait."

Karis, when he found her in carrel eleven the next morning, did not wait for him to finish, and by noon she had a box: a plain pasteboard document box from the stationer's in Ostrand. Into it, in order, went every sheet the provision had ever produced about him: the enrollment record, Gault's baseline note, every drill sheet he had built and every countersignature on each, every floor issue, wrong and revised, every supervised hour since his first week on the bluff. Each was copied in her small hand where the original could not leave the wing, and each copy was dated and initialled and cross-referenced to the original's place in the wing's own files.

"If somebody asks," she said, tying the box's tape in a flat bow, "we hand them this, and it's already in order, and it's already boring. Nobody has ever been made to read a box like this and come out of it angry. They come out of it tired."

"And if nobody asks?"

"Then I've a very tidy box," said Karis, "and I'll sleep better for it, which is worth a morning."

The incident report itself came up through the wing three days later. By then the warden's account ran to four pages. The wing made two copies: faculty, and file. Cael, who read everything on the bluff with his name in it, asked at the counter for the copy he was entitled to, and read it standing at the end of the passage.

It named the pin, the collar, the setting, the nine years, the inspections passed. Its last paragraph asked for every retaining pin in every apparatus on the bluff to be drawn and replaced. The warden wrote the way she spoke, without one spare word, and he copied two of her sentences into the observation notebook to learn from.

He was in it once. Page two, in the warden's account of the afternoon:

*Enrollee (assay provision) present at bay edge; moved to intervene; received the release on a guard arm; no injury reported. Third-year not struck.*

He read it three times. Every word of it was true, and none of it weighed anything. *Moved to intervene.* Six feet in a blink, forward into the hazard, and the hall's own record had two words for it, and both of them were right.

Nobody asked him a single question. Not the warden, not the wing, not Gault. The Ash instructor added a note of three lines to say the enrollee had done well and quickly, and that was true, and it asked nothing. Of all of it, that was what he could not put down. He had spent the whole of his life being asked. On the one afternoon he had done a thing worth asking about, the bluff had written it down in eleven words and turned the page.

---

He watched the coats.

On the first evening after the pin, at the change of the fourth hour, he sat on the coping of the wall behind the second quadrangle with Lira. Together they watched the relief walk up the river street to the ferry landing: two grey coats going, two coming, on the bell, the same as the night before and every night since the bridge.

On the second day the change at the road's foot was four minutes late. He wrote that down and then crossed it out, because one of the relieving pair had stopped on the way to buy a hot pie from a barrow and could be seen eating it.

On the third day there were two and two, on the bell.

Karis came out to the wall that evening with her own log, which she had kept since the bridge in a narrow book with marbled boards: every change at the ferry and the road's foot that any of the four of them had seen, the hour, the faces where faces could be told apart, a tick for on the bell and a small cross for late. She laid it open on the coping between them and ran her finger down a month of columns. Four late changes in a month, all four by under ten minutes, all four with a reason somebody had seen: a pie, a cart in the road, a dog, and once, memorably, a man who had stopped to help an old woman with a basket of eels.

"This is what nothing looks like," said Karis. "I wanted you to see it written down before you start reading something into the next pie."

He studied the columns, which were steadier than his. She had been counting the coats for exactly as long as he had and had never once told him so.

On the morning of the fourth day he woke, and lay still before getting up, and took stock of himself as he did before anything that mattered, and found the hum in his forearm gone. It had not faded. It had simply stopped, sometime in the night, the way a sound stops that has been going on so long you have forgotten to hear it, and the arm under it was only an arm again, a little stiff, a little bruised along the outer bone where the sheet had struck. He wrote: *Compression: the hum, gone on the fourth morning. Arm sound.* Then he went out to watch the coats.

Two and two, on the bell.

"Nothing," said Lira on the coping beside him, that evening.

"Nothing yet."

"You said four days."

"I said a change would show in four days." He watched the relief nod to the two it was relieving, polite as shopkeepers. "I didn't say no change in four days meant nothing went up. A report goes up the coast by courier. Somebody has to read it. Somebody has to decide what it means. Somebody has to decide what to do about what it means." He shook his head. "Four days was the soonest it could show. Not the latest."

"So we don't know."

"We don't know." He wrote the date and *two and two, on the bell*, and closed the notebook. "I'll know more in a week. Or I'll know that I still don't know, which is also something."

Lira looked at him sideways.

"You're very calm about it," she said.

"I'm not calm. I've only got one instrument, and it's slow. Shouting at it won't make it quicker."

On the fifth day the minutes of the faculty's session went up on the board by the theater door, as they always did, and Cael read them because he read everything. Under *apparatus*, Rooke had moved that the floor warden's schedule for replacing every pin on the bluff be adopted in full and at once. The bursar had proposed phasing it over a year. Rooke's ground was minuted in his own words: *the next pin to fail will not wait for the bursar's year*. The motion was carried. Under it, in Bracken's own hand, was a note that the registrar's office would find the money from the reserve within a fortnight and would account for it at the next session.

Jask's story, meanwhile, had grown in the refectory queue exactly as Cael had known it would. By the sixth day the assay boy had crossed the whole hall. By the seventh he had caught the post as it fell, though it had not fallen, and somebody on the lecture stair was telling it with a third-year flung through the air. Brom reported each version at supper with enormous solemnity, like a man reading out the weather from a far country.

"Today you lifted the post over your head," he said on the seventh evening. "With one arm. The bad one."

"Did I put it down again?"

"Nobody knows. The story ends before that."

Jask himself found Cael the next morning at the bottom of the lecture stair, waiting with his hands in his pockets and the look of a man who has been rehearsing a sentence and has lost it on the way.

"I didn't say any of it," he said, before Cael could speak. "The post over your head. The flying. I want you to know. I told it once, the first night, the way it happened, and since then I've not said a word, and it keeps getting bigger anyway." He scowled at the stair. "Somebody in the Force hall asked me yesterday if it was true you'd caught me in the air. I said I was sitting down the whole time. She said I was being modest."

"You were sitting down the whole time."

"I know I was. I was there." Jask rubbed the back of his neck. "I don't want to be the story. I want to be a Force third-year who reads his dial too much. That's all I've ever wanted to be on this hill."

Cael looked at him, and thought of a girl at the fifth bell in front of a board, and of a woman with a brass stud beside her name, and of himself on the lecture stair all season, with ninety people a morning deciding to look. Being the story was a thing that happened to people. Nobody he had ever met had asked for it.

"It'll pass," he said. "Something else will happen. Somebody'll fall off a rope frame, or Brom will walk into another wall."

"He walked into a wall?"

"He'll tell you about it. At length." Cael hesitated, and then gave Jask the one thing he had that might be worth having. "And the dial. You read it after every declaration. If the number's low, you hit the next one harder, and you do that all session, and by the last third you're fighting a needle instead of a post."

Jask stared at him. "How do you know that?"

"I wrote it down. Weeks ago. I write everybody down." He shrugged with his good shoulder. "Turn the dial to the wall for a set. See what you do when you can't see it."

Jask considered this for a long moment with his mouth slightly open, like a man being handed back a coin he had not known he had dropped.

His mouth stayed open a moment. "Odd thing to know about somebody."

"I know," said Cael. "I'm the assay one."

Jask laughed, a short startled bark that echoed up the stair, and went off toward the Force hall shaking his head, and the following week, Cael heard, he turned his dial to the wall for every set and moved up two lines on his own term's ledger, and told nobody why.

---

Eight days after the pin came the fifteenth session, and for a week beforehand there was nobody on the bluff who did not know what it meant.

Lira sent her challenge up to the top line on the morning the cards were made, in her own hand, on the clerk's slip: her name, the arrow, and the name with the brass stud beside it. She did it before breakfast and told nobody, and by the time the three of them came into the refectory, half the room had already read the board.

On paper it was a challenge upward of a single place, the only kind there was, from the second line of the standings to the first. Underneath that it was the seeding of the final, since whoever held the top line at the season's close fought her semifinal on the earlier day and came to the final rested. And underneath that, it was a thing Cael found he could only say plainly in the observation notebook, on the night before, at the foot of three weeks of pages headed *Fiske*.

*Tomorrow is the first time all season either of them meets somebody the ladder didn't hand her. Everyone else Fiske has fought this year, the bracket built for her. Everyone Lira's fought, the bracket built for somebody else. Tomorrow neither of them is what the paper says, and both of them know it, and they're the only two people on the sheet who do.*

Lira found him in the north hall that last evening, after his hour, while he was rolling the cords. She had been running the sequence at half pace on the stone, the short fourth after the half-step, over and over, and she stopped and stood with her towel and asked him one thing.

"How long does she collect?"

"A minute and a half," said Cael. "The same as the first day. Near enough every time. Both her bad openings fall inside it."

"Every time?"

"Four bouts since you sent it. Four pages. A minute and a half, and then the doors start closing." He looked at her. "Why?"

Lira did not answer. She folded the towel in half and in half again, precisely, and hung it over her shoulder, and he saw her lips move once, as Karis's moved before a clause, on a number.

"Go to bed," she said. "You look worse than me."

He had watched Fiske, Force Path, Copper Rank Eight on a certificate two years old, fight four times since Lira's decision, as Lira had wanted him to. Four pages. On every one of them the same thing: a minute and a half of collecting, two bad openings, and then the floor closing behind her opponents like doors in a long corridor, one after another, until there was nowhere left to stand. Fiske never struck anybody. She put her force into the boards where they were about to be, and they arrived on it, and stumbled, and found her hand on them.

On the evening before the bout, crossing the covered walk, Lira and Fiske passed each other under the north stair, in front of the board where both their names stood one above the other.

Cael was ten paces behind and saw it; neither of them stopped walking.

"Fifteenth," said Fiske.

"Fifteenth," said Lira.

That was the whole of it. Fiske went on toward the halls with her hands at the small of her back, and Lira went on toward the residence with her weight on the ball of her right foot, and neither looked round.

The next afternoon the yard was full to the rail.

Cael had never seen it full, though he had seen it crowded, for Nyle and for the Shield fifth-year, with people standing at the backs of the tiers. This was something else. Every tier on all four sides was packed from the lowest step to the rail at the top, and people sat on the steps of the north stair and stood in the archways and leaned out of the windows of the lecture range opposite. The faculty box on the north side had every seat taken and three people standing at its back. The standings clerk, who sat at a small table by the long board for every session of every season, was standing up.

Withrow sat in the west gallery, upright and white-haired, with two people from Ostrand on either side of her whose coats Cael priced, without wanting to, at about a year of residence fees apiece.

He counted, because he could not help counting crowds, the Silver-sheet enrollees he could see on the tiers. Eleven. There had not been more than two at any Copper bout all season.

He did not look for the man with the forms. Twenty-five days now he had kept his eyes off the man. But the brass hood of the lamp hung where it always hung, at the edge of his eye. In its curve the upper east tier lay in afternoon shade, and in the shade, third in a knot of six, a small figure stood with something pale against its chest.

He looked away, at the floor, where the porter was chalking the ring for the last time, slowly, with the face of a man who knew that he was being watched by more people than had ever watched him do anything.

Lira was on the bench at the floor's edge with her towel round her neck and her eyes shut. Brom sat beside Cael on the east tier, very upright, as he sat in church. Karis was on Cael's other side with the grey notebook open on her knee at a fresh page, and she had written the date at the top of it and nothing else, and her pencil was not moving.

Fiske came down the north steps into the sunk floor without hurry, and went to her chalk, and looked at the door frame before she looked at the door.

The adjudicating instructor stood at the table and read the terms in a voice that carried to the rail. A challenge upon the holder's line. Four exchanges, each on the table's glass, each to the cleaner touch or called even. The bout to the practitioner holding more touches at the close of the fourth. A drawn bout to leave the holder where she stood.

The glass was turned, and the sand began to run.

"Begin," said the table.
# Chapter 29 — Four Exchanges

Karis wrote one line for each exchange. Cael read them afterward on her grey page, four lines in her small upright hand, and found that between them they told the whole bout more truly than his six.

*One. L. spends nothing. Why?*

Before the word, Lira did what she had done before every bout since the Ironyard. She crouched at her chalk and laid the flat of one hand on the boards, as if asking them something, and stood, and rolled her neck once each way. The first winter, he had asked why. She had said it was so her hand would know the floor before her feet had to, and had refused to say another word on the subject for two years. Fiske, across the chalk, kept her hands clasped behind her and studied the boards between them, as a carpenter studies a frame before hanging the door in it.

At the table's word, Fiske gave ground.

She always did; Cael had four pages that said so. Her art lived in arriving early, at the place another fighter was about to be, and she could not arrive early anywhere until she knew the fighter, so for the first stretch of every bout she was only learning, and learning made her clumsy. She stepped back off her line in the first breaths, and then again, nearly over her own chalk. Two openings, exactly as the pages promised, and on the tiers a hundred people sat forward at once.

Lira let both of them go by.

She did not even look at them. She stood on her chalk for a long moment after the word, quite still, with her weight on the ball of her right foot. Then she began, at a walk, to run her evasion sequence.

Cael knew it the instant he saw the first step. Everybody who had ever trained beside her knew it. It was the drill she ran on the north hall's stone at half pace whenever her hip would let her do nothing else: eleven movements, always in the same order, a step left and a check, a turn, two steps right along an arc, a fold and a rise, round and back to where she began. She had run it ten thousand times. She ran it now on the yard's boards in front of eight hundred people at the pace of somebody showing a child how to tie a knot, with no Wind in it anywhere, and when she reached the end she began it again from the start.

Fiske watched it.

Cael watched Fiske watch it, and felt the back of his neck go cold. Lira was teaching her. Step by step, slowly enough for anyone to follow, she was laying out the order her feet liked to go in and the places they liked to go, and the woman across from her, who lived on knowing where people would be, was taking down every word. He saw Fiske's head tilt a hair at the turn. He saw her weight shift a hair toward the arc before Lira reached it, the second time round. The third time, Fiske's eyes were on the place where the fold would come before Lira had begun the step that led to it.

Lira tested her guard twice in passing, a hand going out low, a hand going out high, with nothing behind either. They were only part of the drill.

The sand ran down, and at the table every eye went to the glass.

"Even," said the instructor. "No touch. First exchange."

A groan went round the tiers like wind through standing corn, the noise of people who have paid for a fight and been shown a dance. Behind Cael somebody said distinctly, *she's thrown that away*. Down on the south tier the cohort sat in their dark knot and looked at one another and did not seem sure.

"Why?" said Karis, beside him, very low. The pencil sat idle where the line ended.

"She asked me last night how long Fiske collects." Cael kept his eyes on the boards. "I told her a minute and a half. So she gave her two minutes. She gave her everything she wanted to know, and every bit of it was true. That's her sequence. That's what she does when she's left alone."

Karis's gaze went from him to the page and stayed there, and the pencil did not move again.

*Two. F. closes the doors. F. one.*

---

Fiske took the second exchange, and took it the way a locksmith takes a door.

At the word, Lira went into the sequence again, at speed now, the first step left and the check. Fiske was already there. Not at Lira. At the place the step after the check would have gone. Her declaration went into the boards there, short and flat, without any show at all, and the boards there turned to something no foot could trust, a smooth hard slickness lying over the oak like oil on a stair. Lira saw it and broke off the turn.

Fiske had already shut the arc.

A second declaration, three beats after the first, into the place the sequence went next. Lira broke off again, and the third went down behind her, into the stretch of floor the fold would have brought her through, and in eleven seconds there was nowhere on the boards that the sequence could reach. Lira stood in a narrow patch of honest wood with oil on three sides of it, and every edge of the patch had been drawn by Fiske, from the copy Lira herself had handed her a minute earlier.

Cael found that he was gripping the binder hard enough to bend its board.

He had charted Force on five floors. Nobody he had charted could have done that, and she had not hit anybody. She had read a page and closed it, line by line, faster than the girl who had written it could turn over.

He wrote it down, honestly, on Fiske's page and not Lira's, because a page that only ever praised his friends would have been no better than a letter home.

*She isn't fighting Lira. She's fighting the drill Lira showed her. She's right about every step of it, because every step of it was true.*

Lira went for the last open side on her own feet, quick and low, and Fiske was there before her. Her flat hand landed on Lira's shoulder.

"Force. One."

On the tiers a long breath went out, and somebody behind Cael said *there*, in the satisfied voice of a man who has seen a picture straightened on a wall.

"One to none," said Brom, from Cael's left. His voice was very quiet. "Two left."

"She needs both," said Karis. "If it ends level, Fiske keeps the line."

"I know," said Brom. "I'm only saying two is a lot."

Lira walked back to her chalk. At the turn she glanced down at the slick patches already fading from the boards, and then, for an instant, across at the place behind Fiske where nothing had been closed.

*Three. L. runs the sequence and lies at four places. F.'s fourth goes into the floor. One each.*

---

At the word, Lira went into the sequence again.

The same first step left, the same check, and Fiske put her declaration into the place where the turn came next, as she had in the second exchange, and Lira's turn did not come. A burst came instead, the first Wind of the bout, and it took her not round the turn but straight past it, a stride beyond where the sequence had ever gone in ten thousand runs, onto clean wood.

Fiske's force lay on the oak behind her, a slick patch on a stretch of floor nobody was standing on.

The arc next. Fiske shut it, from the copy, a beat early, exactly right. The second burst took Lira out of the arc's middle in the wrong direction, inward instead of out, toward Fiske, which the drill had never once done. Fiske's force went down on the arc's far end and found nothing on it.

Then the fold.

The fold was where the sequence always paused, a half-breath down and up. It was also, Cael saw with a shock that went through his own hip, where a third burst would end if a fighter had taken two before it: the exact place the cohort's plan had marked, the third landing, the beat when a Wind fighter on this sheet stood locked and spent in front of a fresh opponent. Every fighter in the Copper column knew that beat now, Fiske among them. Her third declaration was already going down under it.

Lira burst into it. Then, from inside the last of the landing beat, she took a half-step.

It was nothing. A shift of one foot sideways, short, with no Wind in it, so small that most of the yard never saw it and those who did thought she had slipped. It moved her a foot, and Fiske's third went into the boards a foot from Lira's heel instead of under it.

The fourth burst came from there, from a place nobody counting landings would ever have stood to wait.

Cael felt all four go in his own leading hip, forty feet up. Four held breaths, not one returned; the last she paid for with breath borrowed from tomorrow. Four landing beats, four half-breaths locked in place, every one of them standing still in front of the one woman on the bluff who could hit a still thing before it knew it was still. And the hip. From the tier he watched the leading side take the load, and take it, and take it again, and the line it was drawing down her thigh was one he had drawn in himself at two bursts and never dared carry to four.

All four went to a single purpose. She had run her own sequence, the true one, the one she had shown Fiske, and lied with it at four places. Four times Fiske's force had gone where the copy said the girl must be. Four times the girl had gone somewhere else, not by any trick of her weight or her lean, but because the burst did not come out of her weight or her lean at all.

Fiske's fourth declaration was building.

Cael saw her see it: shoulders squaring, the force gathering, and the place it was for already empty. She stopped it. A Force practitioner can stop, if she will pay everything she has built to do it, and the force has to go somewhere when she does. Fiske sent it down. It went into the oak at her own feet with a flat, wrong crack, like a beam splitting in a roof, and eight hundred people drew one breath at once.

Lira's hand was on Fiske's ribs before the sound had finished.

"Wind. One each."

*Four. F. stops placing. L. walks in. L. two to one.*

Neither of them had anything left to stand behind, and both of them knew it.

Fiske could not read the girl any more. Whatever she had been reading had stopped meaning what it said, and she knew it. Cael watched the knowledge settle into the line of her mouth as she walked back to her chalk. So at the word she did the only thing left to a champion whose instrument has been broken over her head in front of her whole hill. She stopped placing. She built, and committed, and came straight at Lira with a plain heavy delivery, the Force that every other Force fighter on the sheet had always used.

Not one bout all season had she fought like the rest of the column. Now she did, and from her, even so, it was frightening.

Four bursts gone, the whole of her ceiling. If one remained, it would be slow, and short, and paid for all week. Cael sat on the tier with his hands locked on the binder and did the sum and came to that number just before Lira came to the moment of needing it.

She did not need it.

She stepped in on her own feet. Not the Wind's feet: the feet a foundry floor at Ardenmere had given her over two winters, when she had been younger and poorer and had learned that a burst you can't pay for is a burst you haven't got. She went toward the delivery at a slant, so that its edge caught her across the left shoulder instead of its whole weight across the chest. It hit her hard; her knees dipped and the last of her breath went out of her in a short hard sound. She came up out of the dip with no distance left between them at all, inside Fiske's recovery, with her palm flat on Fiske's breastbone.

Four seconds. Close and graceless, and as plain as a signature.

"Wind," said the table. "Two to one. Bout."

The yard came apart.

Not slowly, as it had after Nyle. It went up all at once, every tier, so that the noise of it hit the lecture range opposite and came back off the stone. In the faculty box a grey-bearded member of the law range had stood up and was clapping with his hands over his head like a boy at a fair. Cael was on his feet without having decided to stand. Brom beside him had both fists pressed hard against his mouth. And Karis, on his other side, sat perfectly upright with the pencil in her fingers, writing nothing, with tears running down her face that she did not appear to have noticed.

Down on the boards, Lira stood where she had touched, her hand still half raised, until the safety instructor walked out and laid his own hand very gently on her arm.

In the west gallery Withrow had not stood. She sat exactly as she had sat all afternoon, upright and white-haired, with her two Ostrand guests on their feet on either side of her, clapping. She was not watching the floor. She was watching their faces, the way she always watched the faces beside her on these afternoons, and from the east tier Cael saw her turn her head and say something short to the woman on her left, who nodded slowly, twice, and took a small book out of her coat and wrote in it.

And in the brass of the lamp, high on the east side, in the shade, a small figure with something pale against its chest had not moved at all, through any of it. Cael saw that without looking for it, and let it go.

---

It took Cael most of a quarter-hour to get down through the crowd on the stair. When he reached the floor's edge he found them both still there.

Lira sat two steps up from the boards, bent over her knees, taking her breath back a mouthful at a time. The leading leg was folded under her at an angle he did not like, and she had not tried to stand. She was keeping her face entirely empty of that, and of everything else, in front of the boards.

Fiske had a cup of water in her hand. She crossed to the step and stood over Lira.

By the long board, threescore people found the chalk suddenly fascinating.

Fiske spoke first. "You gave me a false first exchange."

Lira raised her head.

"I gave you a true one. You just assumed it was all of them."

Fiske's face changed.

Cael knew how a beaten champion's face went. This was not that. Cael had watched that one all season, on Fiske's opponents, and knew it to the eyelid. This was what he had seen once before and failed to name: at the board in the first month, the transfer with the flag, Fiske with a towel round her neck, standing two breaths longer than the words needed. That afternoon he had failed to name it. He read it now from four paces away, entire, and wondered how he had ever missed it.

It was relief. Both times, it had been relief.

"There's a form," said Fiske. "For an advancement evaluation. The registry sends an Arbiter if the form goes up. It wants one signature that isn't mine." She turned the cup in her hands. "Two seasons I've sat on top of that sheet, with Rank Eight on my certificate. Two crowns. Every bout on this hill worth having. The porter polishes the stud beside my name on Fourth-days. And in all that time nobody has picked up the pen. Not because anyone decided not to. Because nobody ever had a reason to. I was winning. Why would anybody send the winner away?"

Lira said nothing. Every bit of her attention was on Fiske.

Fiske looked down at her. "So win it. Win the crown. Then make them *look* at you. It's the looking that matters — it was always the looking. The crown's just how you force it."

Fiske drank, and then studied what was left in the cup.

"I didn't know how. I thought if I won enough, it would turn into a reason on its own. It doesn't turn into anything. They chalk it up, and once a thing's chalked up, people feel they've dealt with it."

Her gaze went past Lira to the long board. The brass stud still sat beside her name, a line lower than it had sat at noon. Until the final, it was still hers.

"Six weeks," said Fiske. "I'll see you in the final."

"You will," said Lira.

She held out her free hand. Lira looked at it a moment and took it, and Fiske did not pull her up by it, as a lesser fighter might have done in front of the boards to make a show of being gracious. One shake, and she let go.

Fiske walked away along the bottom of the tiers, cup in hand. Round the board, the onlookers began all at once to discuss the weather.

Lira stayed on the step a while longer. Then she put a hand flat on the stone and pushed herself up, slowly, in two stages, and Cael kept his own hands where they were. In front of the boards she would have refused his help, and he had known that since the Ironyard.

---

That night Brom did his fourth exercise of the week at the post behind the second quadrangle, by lamplight, because Karis had found him at supper and said the date aloud.

Cael fed him. Karis had the coping, the lamp and the grey notebook. And Lira, who should have been in bed with her leg up, sat on the coping beside Karis with her leg out straight along the stone and a wet cloth folded over the hip, and watched Brom hold and shift and hold and shift, forty times, with his heel trying to lift on every one and being set down again by main force.

On the thirty-first, without any warning, she said it.

She said all of Fiske's words in order, about the form and the signature and the stud and the pen, and the four sentences in the middle, and the board, and *six weeks*. She said them to the dark beyond the lamp in Fiske's flat voice, as though setting down something she had been asked to carry across a room. It took her the better part of a minute. She did not drop a single word. Brom stopped holding, and Cael stopped feeding. Nobody wrote anything down while she talked; Cael had learned that much at Ardenmere, in the first month, and had not forgotten it.

When she stopped, the lamp hissed.

"All of it?" said Cael.

"All of it. She said each thing once. There wasn't any show in it." Lira moved the cloth. "I've heard people say true things before. Plenty. I've never heard anybody put down a thing they'd had to carry by themselves for two years. She'd been carrying it around looking for somebody who could take it off her."

"And it was you."

"It was the first time anybody on that sheet was able to." She said it without any pride, the way she would have told him the time. "That's an ugly thing to find out about where you live."

Karis had the pencil out. "May I write it down? Exactly as you said it."

"You can write it down exactly if you never call it *the file*. Not once. Not to anybody."

"I won't," said Karis, and wrote, and didn't.

Lira looked out at the dark where the ferry landing was, and the two grey coats under their lamp, on the bell.

"And now I like her," she said. "I've no idea where to put that."

"Nothing," said Brom.

He was still at the post with his forearms up and the sweat running into his eyes, on the thirty-first of forty, with Rooke's sheet folded in his back pocket and a row of Karis's ticks on it, and a row of small crosses in his own hand beside them for the days he had wanted to stop and hadn't.

"You beat her," said Brom. "And then she told you something true. That's her being a good sort. You don't have to file it."

"Karis is going to file it."

"Karis files everything. That's Karis being a good sort." He set his heel down hard and shifted his weight. "Thirty-two. Feed me, Cael, before my arms forget what they're for."

Cael fed him. Karis, between ticks, turned back two pages in the grey notebook and held it out across the coping to Lira, open at the four lines she had written on the tier.

Lira read them by the lamp, and Cael watched her eyes go down the page and stop at the first line, at the question mark, and stay there.

"Why," said Lira.

"I didn't know," said Karis. "Not then. Cael told me on the tier, and I still didn't really know till the third." Back came the notebook; Karis frowned at her own question mark. "I wanted to cross it out after. I thought it looked stupid. Then I thought it was the most honest thing on the page, because it's what eight hundred people were thinking, and I'm the only one who wrote it down."

"Leave it," said Lira. "It's a good question. Fiske asked it too. For the whole minute and a half." She shifted the wet cloth on her hip and looked at Brom straining at the post, his heel lifting and being forced down. "She just didn't get the answer till it was too late to use. Neither did you."

"Did you know?" said Brom, through his teeth, on thirty-five. "Before the word?"

"I knew what I meant to do." Lira considered. "I didn't know if it would work. You never know that. You only know if you've built it so that if it doesn't work, you'll find out early."

"That's Rooke," said Brom.

"That's the foundry," said Lira. "Rooke just says it in fewer words."

---

It was very late when he opened the Log for the session. The river was loud below the window and the lamp turned all the way up.

He put the fragments first, as he had at the head of every session entry since Ardenmere, one line to each.

*Five confirmed fragments. All five as they were.*

Then one line he had been carrying in the second column for most of three weeks, and now, with a certain ceremony, ruled through and copied into the first.

*Iron-adjacent read. Off the counting four days, back to full: three times running, four days each time. Then under load at the post in hall three, seventeen days rested, on time and true when everybody else in the hall was a beat behind. Confirmed. The watching and the read drink from one well. Four days fills it.*

Then the bout. He found he did not want to write it as he usually wrote a bout, exchange by exchange, because Karis had already done that in four lines and done it better. He wrote what it had been instead.

*Fifteenth session. Two to one on the final's terms; second line to first on the standings. Nine and nothing on the season now: nine bouts unbeaten, none lost, the opening bout drawn by lot among them.*

*Every exchange took one more pretence off. In the first, Lira showed Fiske the truth about herself and nothing else. In the second, Fiske believed it and was right to. In the third, Lira used the same truth to go somewhere it couldn't have told anybody. By the fourth there was nothing left to show or believe, only two people, and Lira's feet had been made on a foundry floor.*

*What the third one cost her, in my own hip's language. Four bursts, all borrowed against tomorrow. Four half-breaths stood still in front of the one fighter on the bluff who could have used any of them. A hip I'd guess at three days before it lets her walk, and then the semifinal on top of it. A left shoulder that took the edge of a Force delivery so she wouldn't have to give up a hand's breadth of distance.*

*What it bought. The top line and the earlier semifinal, so a day's more rest before the final. And a woman who's held the crown two years walking across the floor to give her the one honest thing anybody on this hill has said aloud since we came up the road.*

*And the other column. On the morning after the final, Bracken's sheet will still say Copper, Rank Two, in the tier column beside her name. Fiske has already said what the crown is for, better than I could. I'll only write down the word for it. The crown is a lever.*

He read it through. One thing had lain under all the rest of the day, waiting. He gave it the foot of the page, in a smaller hand.

*At the fifth bell, most mornings, she's at the board alone. She's been going for weeks and never mentioned it. The morning after the tenth, I came up the north stair and found her there in front of six and nothing, standing square on both feet with her breathing held level, which is how she stands when a thing is costing her.*

*I went back down without speaking. I keep going back over it. I think I was right to. I'm not certain, and I want the not-being-certain on the page. The day I'm sure I know when somebody wants to be left alone is the day I've started deciding for people what they need. I've spent my life being decided for. I know what it's like from the inside.*

He blotted the page and closed the Log and sat a long while with his hands on its cover, listening to the river.

Somewhere along the passage, through the thin wall, he heard Lira laugh once in her sleep, short and surprised, and then go quiet.

