| Opening pull | 7/10 | Ch. 30, opening through “a man who sent everything down the river would never need a drawer of his own” | The secret report and hidden case promise danger, but the initial file-and-product reasoning is quiet and terminology-heavy before Cael appears. | Medium-high |
| Keep reading | 9/10 | Ch. 34, from “The door stood open. A man was standing in it” through the `FRAGMENT ACQUIRED` notice | The vigil pays off with a vivid confrontation, a new fragment, and immediate uncertainty about its cost. The unidentified client and coming evaluation keep pressure alive. | High |
| Interest / freshness | 9/10 | Ch. 33, “He isn’t stopping them looking. He’s making the look go bankrupt” | Surveillance is treated as a learnable, physical contest of attention rather than generic invisibility. The fire-watch, oil slate, loose stair, and paperwork make the problem specific. | High |
| Clarity / flow | 7/10 | Ch. 30, “below the standing line for the five confirmed fragments”; Ch. 31, “Earnest, in his own work, close: that’s your rule two” | Scene action is clear, especially in the night watch, but inherited system terms and prior agreements arrive faster than a cold reader can fully rank them. Meaning catches up through repetition. | High |
| Character attachment | 9/10 | Ch. 31, the four-person signed contingency; Ch. 37, the birthday table and Hesk’s pen | Cael admits dangerous wants; his friends answer with rules, food, coats, measurements, and remembered dates. Care is demonstrated through action. | High |
