books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:4:**Cael's age: sixteen at open (verified against Book 4's close — the on-page birthday was B4 Chapter 14); turns SEVENTEEN on-page in Chapter 14 — the series' second staged birthday, deliberately rhymed with Book 4's, deliberately smaller. No silent drift: every age reference before Ch14 reads sixteen, every reference after reads seventeen**
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:75:The summer recess, staged in the log-voice opening movement the way Book 4 staged its skip — dated, inventoried, done: three months of training on a quieter campus; letters (Hesk's arriving fast as ever); the watchers outside the walls holding their rotation with the patience of men paid by the year; Seln at his desk the whole recess, which nobody remarks on and everybody notices; Karis spending the break doing what Karis does with a banked clause — building the argument around it until the argument can stand cross-examination. Cael is sixteen, and says so on the page. Six confirmed fragments. One anomaly. One semester stamp. The book opens on the first day of the qualifying year.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:167:The early rounds run: Lira and Karis through their opening Iron bouts (separate pools — the chapter confirms the draw's geometry on-page so the reader never wonders why they don't meet), Brom cutting through Copper openers, Ephram solid into the Iron round of sixteen. Cael's exhibition slate posts with two volunteer challenges — both Silver-tier, both curious, scheduled for Chapter 13 — and the Concourse's rumor engine celebrates: the demonstration category hasn't drawn Silver volunteers in living memory. *Key beat (banked, quietly):* Karis, reading the team-trial rules in preparation for the squad phase, stops on the eligibility text — *squads shall be drawn from the academy's roster* — no tier qualifier, no classification requirement. She reads it twice, looks at nothing in particular, and copies it into the notebook beside the fielding clause with one word: "Again." (Not acted on. Not yet. Chapter 15's premise, banked exactly as the Book 4 note was banked.)
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:222:Hesk's package, held ready as always: a bound blank ledger volume, instrument-maker's work, and the note in full: *"Seventeen. My trade says an instrument is finished when it measures true — not when it looks done. You measure true. Let the rest of them look. — H."* Cael is seventeen, on the page, witnessed, the date written into the log in his own hand. The inventory follows in the new volume's first entry: six confirmed fragments — Wind, Pressure, Iron, Compression, Ember, Shadow — deployment notes current; one anomaly, session nine, Tide-adjacent, turned over once, closed with the standing three words: *Still open. Still real. Patience.*
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:259:**Halcenvane places third in the Continental Academy Tournament.** The standings land on-page with the math the season built: Brom's Copper title, Lira's Iron semifinal, Karis's quarterfinal run, Ephram's round of sixteen, team trial third. For a mid-tier academy three years into Withrow's program, it is a triumph with no asterisk — the chapter gives the delegation its night, whole and earned, before the book turns to the match the standings can't hold. Withrow's toast is in her flattest registry-official register and lands like a flag planted: "Third on the continent, by the instrument's own arithmetic. Whatever else happens on finals day — they counted us. Remember when they wouldn't."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:279:The strategic heart of the chapter is the banking decision, staged as the formal end of an era: surviving Gold requires everything, and Cael — with the inner circle, on the page, in the log — spends the bank. Wind, Pressure, Iron, Compression, Ember: full public deployment authorized for the first time in his life. One reserve held, deliberately, and the holding *named*: Shadow-adjacent stays sealed — not because it would not help, but because it is the one fragment whose exposure burns someone else's cover, and the debt he owes its source does not suspend for a Gold match. (Seln is not told he is the reason. The asymmetry-debt beat, carried forward from B4 Ch14, honored and still unpaid.) Seln's actual contribution arrives as tradecraft, unsigned, in the coursework-folder register of B4 Ch20: *Judges rewatch what they cannot score. Whatever you show tomorrow will be studied frame by frame by people whose profession is categories. Decide tonight what you are content to have studied forever.* Cael burns the note. Then he sits with the log a long time and writes the entry the arc requires: "Quenna asked if I could not-do it in front of people who wish me harm. I learned to. Tomorrow's question is new: whether I can afford to keep not-doing it in front of someone who can kill me by accident. I don't think I can. Write it down before, not after."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:341:The ledger of the year, in its established forms. The Power Log inventory, in Hesk's new volume: **seven confirmed fragments** — Wind-adjacent, Pressure-adjacent, Iron-adjacent, Compression-adjacent, Ember-adjacent, Shadow-adjacent, Storm-adjacent, the last still flagged *stability: provisional*, its integration cost logged honestly across the post-match week (the frameworks resettling slower than after any acquisition before it; Gold architecture is heavy) — and the anomaly, session nine, Tide-adjacent, turned over once, the standing three words. One log line for an old promise: "Reydan will read the accounts. It still isn't the answer. Not yet." Letters: to Hesk (whose reply, fast as ever, reads in full: *"Third on the continent, and the judges wrote 'no number goes here.' Your grandmother would have had that framed. Seventeen, unmeasured, and true. Come home when the road allows. — H."*); to Vell, because her ledger still holds his first honest record and she will want the tournament told straight. Brom's advancement review: scheduled, pending, unresolved on the page. Lira, at the rail, watching Auremont's banner: "Silver bracket. Next cycle." Nobody at the rail says what the reader and Vastin already suspect about whether the official channels will last that long. The road home from Norhold: the six of them — Seln traveling with the delegation, at the table now, which no one remarks on and everyone counts.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:353:- [ ] **Book 4 → Book 5 seam verified against Book 4's architecture close:** Book 4 ends at semester's close — Cael sixteen, six confirmed fragments + Tide anomaly, assay enrollment renewed by the Ch21 evaluation, Lira formally Iron-tier Rank 1 (Ch24 Arbiter advancement), Brom's force-redirect flaw repaired (Ch19), Karis's tournament-clause note banked with one word ("Interesting."), Seln functionally switched and still posted, Vastin's file note filed (*Classification error is unlikely. Continue observation.*), Withrow's tournament program declared, qualifying season "next year." Book 5 honors all of it: the ~three-month skip is explicit and staged in Chapter 1; the fielding clause is deployed as banked (Ch1–2) and its text matches Book 4 Ch24 verbatim (*participating academies shall field any practitioner appearing on their enrollment record*); Lira competes in the Iron bracket AS Iron R1; Brom's repaired defense is load-bearing in Ch11; the Vastin relationship builds strictly from the Ch22-B4 civil interview baseline — nothing warmer, nothing colder, until the Ch23 warning changes the terms honestly.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:354:- [ ] **Age handled deliberately — no silent drift:** Cael is sixteen at Chapter 1 (stated on the page) per Book 4's on-page Ch14 birthday. He turns SEVENTEEN on-page in Chapter 14 — the series' second staged birthday, smaller by design (Hesk's ledger-volume gift, the letter in full, the date written into the log). Every reference before Ch14 reads sixteen; every reference after reads seventeen; the Ch16 match posting and all finals-day material read seventeen. The bible's Arc 2 age band (17–19) remains PROVISIONAL and drifted; this book continues the drafted timeline (B2–B4 verified) — flag for the future bible reconciliation pass, do NOT retrofit.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:355:- [ ] **SECRET discipline — the effect goes public, the mechanism does not:** The Book 3 SECRET (integration of witnessed abilities) remains known ONLY to the inner circle — Lira, Brom, Karis. What the Daeva match publishes is an inexplicable EVENT: a practitioner with no Storm record deploying Storm architecture mid-bout. No character outside the circle names, guesses, or approaches the mechanism; the public interpretations (Ch22) are enumerated and all wrong; the words "integration," "fragment," and "acquisition" never leave the log and the circle. **Seln does NOT learn the mechanism this book** — he witnesses the public event like everyone at Norhold; the asymmetry-debt (B4 Ch14) is re-surfaced in Ch19 (Shadow-adjacent held sealed to protect him, without his knowledge) and remains unpaid — available to the Book 6 era when he formally comes inside. Book 6's falsification SECRET and Book 8's Architect SECRET: untouched. Daeva learns only what she saw ("That was mine." / "A piece of it.") — an honest shape, no mechanism.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:358:- [ ] **Fragment count at book close:** 7 confirmed integrations — Wind-adjacent (B1, Lira), Pressure-adjacent (B1, Feryn), Iron-adjacent (B2 Ch13, Brom), Compression-adjacent (B2 Ch24, Reydan), Ember-adjacent (B3 Ch13, Karis), Shadow-adjacent (B4 Ch13, Seln), **Storm-adjacent (B5 Ch21, Daeva — first Gold-equivalent source, first stability-flagged notice, first acquisition witnessed publicly)**. Plus one anomaly entry (Tide-adjacent, B2 Ch19) — referenced ONLY in Ch14 and Ch24 inventories with the standing three words; NOT reproduced, NOT confirmed, NOT advanced, never counted as an eighth fragment. Trajectory check against bible: Book 6 close requires nine integrated abilities — 7 at B5 close leaves exactly two for Book 6's architecture to stage. Shadow-adjacent deployed NOWHERE public this book (Ch19 decision, Ch20–21 held) — the banking of the other five is formally spent (Ch19–20), a permanent change to his public record that Book 6 inherits.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:359:- [ ] **Companion status at book close:** Lira — Wind Path, Iron-tier Rank 1 formal; continental Iron semifinalist; Fenmark closed as a wound (Ch6); Silver-bracket ambition declared on-page (Ch10, Ch24) — the trajectory Book 6's walkout will cost her, deliberately built here. Brom — Iron Skin Path, Copper-tier formal; **continental Copper champion**; mandatory advancement review scheduled and PENDING at close (unresolved by design — Book 6 walkout stake); Velmere letter beat (sister's hand, one line) touched and banked, family thread still open. Karis — Ember Path, Iron-tier Rank 3; quarterfinal win over Ternhall's top rank by leaving her own documentation; both clause-technicalities (fielding, squad eligibility) hers. Seln — Shadow Path, Bronze-tier; full companion in function, Compact TA in form; sat at the table (Ch14) and traveled home with them (Ch24) — one step past B4's wall, no confession, no ceremony; null reports maintained; cache untouched; still posted at Halcenvane (Book 6 needs the desk to walk away from). Ephram — arc completed to open ally (Ch8, Ch15); remains at Halcenvane, available to Book 6.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-08.md:3:The road to Norhold ran seventeen days, and it ran downhill in every sense Cael had words for: out of the hills with the spring melt, down the Ost's widening valley past Ostrand — where the whole town, primed by a winter of circulars, turned out at the bridge to see its academy's delegation pass — and then north and east along roads that grew a lane at a time, past waystations that became coaching towns, coaching towns that became river ports, until the traffic itself changed species and the delegation's two wagons were a small fact in a stream of freight and banners and other academies' columns all flowing the same direction, like a watershed running backward, everything on the continent that could move draining toward one city.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-08.md:103:Ephram's pool read hard and fair — a first-round bout against the coastal column's second entry, a probable round-of-sixteen meeting with the lake academy's methodical Shield, and a pool line that kept him clear of both Lira and Karis until a hypothetical final. He read it twice, nodded once, and produced his verdict in the cohort's economic register: "Nothing free, nothing unfair. Correct tournament." Then, because the new Ephram carried the old one's honesty upgraded rather than replaced: "Round of sixteen is my price of admission. Everything past it is profit, and I intend to trade."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-18.md:9:Auremont had held her from the year before — recruited off the feeder school's first assessments, kept close, waiting for the hour — and the continental program had her within the season of it. And the ladder — this was the part the file recorded as triumph and she had stopped, somewhere in her teens, being able to read as anything but what it was — the ladder had come to her. Accelerated cohorts, assembled around her age and rebuilt twice as she outgrew them. Exhibition schedules, adjusted annually to keep her opposition meaningful as the meaningful pool shrank. Instructors imported, facilities extended, her advancement evaluations conducted by senior Arbiters at stations that opened early for the purpose. Iron at fourteen. Silver at sixteen. Gold at seventeen — the youngest in continental history, the certification ceremony attended by delegates from institutions she had never visited, her Kindling anniversary by then a minor Auremont holiday, observed with a half-day and a demonstration. Gold Rank 3 at nineteen. Youngest at that threshold too. Youngest at every threshold, forever, a row of records with her name filling a page of the registry's honors annex.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-18.md:89:The circuit had its stations. At the west quarter she stopped at the arrestor mast and laid two fingers against its grounded cable with the proprietary distaste of a person inspecting her own cage. "They rate these off my assessment ceilings," she said. "Every venue I've fought since I was sixteen has ordered them. Do you know what it does to a bout, fighting inside apparatus built to catch your mistakes?" — and answered herself before he could, because the answer predated him: "It teaches everyone watching that your mistakes are a civic event. So you stop making them. Not fewer. *None*. Four years, none." She released the cable. "The dossier says you lose sometimes. Circuit bouts, early. A fair-day fighter named Sarel." She looked at him with the first plain curiosity of the evening. "What is it like?"
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-09.md:47:Ephram beat the coastal column's second entry in a bout Rooke used for the evening's teaching — "watch the third exchange; he spent two flags to learn the man's reset and bought the bout with the knowledge; that's the trade, that's the whole trade" — and stood, by the second evening, in the round of sixteen, at his stated price of admission, trading.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-19.md:81:"Shadow-adjacent stays sealed."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-19.md:83:The inner circle absorbed it, and Lira — because the council's forms required the argument and she was the one who could make it hurt — made it aloud: "Say the price of the seal, then. Presence-suppression against a fighter whose whole game is reading your position. Movement-masking against corridor-seeding. It's the one architecture in the vault built for exactly her fight, and you're proposing to leave it home." She wasn't wrong, and the room knew it, and she finished the argument herself, because she had run its other half before she started: "And the answer is the provenance. Say it anyway. Rooms remember what gets said in them." They knew the fragment's provenance, and the provenance was the point. Presence-suppression and movement-masking, acquired sixteen months ago from a source whose entire life was the concealment of what he was. A Shadow-architecture deployment, in the most-documented bout in tournament history, before recording scribes and category professionals and a Compact row at full attendance, would be studied frame by frame forever — and somewhere in that study, someone whose profession was patterns would ask where an unclassified enrollee had witnessed Shadow Path work sustained enough to matter, and the question would begin walking, and every road it could walk ended at a mild administrative man whose cover was his life.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-006.md:30:- **Ephram's round-of-sixteen exit (~1,400), new on the page:** the lake academy's methodical Shield. His stated price of admission is paid in full, and he says so first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:31:- **Eve:** the third form of the found family's night-before. Karis's model takes four hours: the honesty sheet; the first model dies at Brom's objection, the second at Lira's ("My hair didn't move"); "she arrives third"; *permitted*; the boxed half-beat; the card burned. Rooke's padwork, eleven short. Brom's survival frame and codicil ("going down well is a skill"). Lira's liturgy. **The banking council** (forms, Karis argues the alternative at full strength, the minute written before the fact) and **Shadow held sealed** for the debt (Lira states the price). **Seln's slip** (Seln cutaway ≈1,000: writing it, squaring it, nothing else) and the stove protocol with ash already broken. The house at night. The Quenna entry, verbatim.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:40:- **The council:** the mechanism is spoken only among the four. Seln and Ephram are outside the door. Shadow is sealed, and **Seln is never told why** (B6 Ch15 is where he learns).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-001.md:13:It is the last night of a twelve-week summer recess at Halcenvane, on the bluff above Ostrand. Book 4 closed on a wall above the river: six things that aren't a Path, a stamp that says he proved it, and a teaching assistant sixty feet along the wall saying nothing, "which — from him — was agreement." Since then Cael has trained sixty-one sessions and the Wind ceiling held at six. Hesk has written nine letters. The watchers kept their rotation. Seln sat at a summer counter all recess. Karis spent ten weeks building the argument around a clause she banked with one word. Cael is sixteen and says so. Open in the log, at the window, then go to convocation.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-001.md:17:Put Cael on a continental roster by the plain operation of other people's honest text, and teach the instrument the whole book is about. This is the book's densest teaching stretch, at a target of about 83 progression-vocabulary hits per 10k words. Teach tier brackets seeded by paper, the demonstration provision, the exhibition category, the registry's validation, and the Silver Standard's architecture: execution, control and effect; five judges with the high and low struck; scaled to a Silver Rank 5 par. Teach Cael's six fragments, each with its standing cost. The reader should finish able to guess what a rating will say before a panel posts it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-001.md:20:- **The recess closes in the log** (inventory, Hesk's "Sleep now. You won't later."). Dramatize one dawn ceiling session in the last week, enough to show that the cost structure is the work, not the number. Show the Shadow fragment's idle cost and drift count as a background tax.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-24.md:43:*Seven confirmed fragments. Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — Feryn. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — held sealed through the most public season of my life; deployment still none, anywhere, ever; the debt still unpaid and still unspoken. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. First fragment from a Gold-equivalent source. First notice to carry a stability flag, and the flag has earned its keep: integration cost logged honestly across the post-match week — the frameworks resettling slower than after any acquisition before it, the Wind economy still returning to rate, the whole architecture re-seating around the new mass like a house settling on a heavier stone. Gold architecture is heavy. The ledger notes it the way Hesk would: a true instrument records its own strain.*
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-24.md:79:The road home from Norhold ran seventeen days in reverse, and the delegation's two wagons took it loaded with a season — the standard, the plaque, the files, the kit — and the six of them, because it was six now.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-24.md:87:Nobody remarked on it, at that waystation or any of the sixteen after. Everybody counted it, every night, the way you count the members of a family at a door.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-14.md:111:*Six fragments. Wind-adjacent — Lira; the oldest; working ceiling six repetitions, deployment notes current through the Shield bout's eleven relocations, which taxed the ceiling's economics a new way and taught me displacement is cheaper than evasion at scale. Pressure-adjacent — Feryn; reliable, restricted, the restriction now three years old and load-bearing in the record. Iron-adjacent — Brom; the surface read; the cheapest thing I own and the one that has paid for every Silver exchange of this tournament. Compression-adjacent — Reydan; contact absorption; banked in public since the day it arrived, held banked at a handspan's range twice this fortnight, at a price the old volume records honestly. Ember-adjacent — Karis, in her handwriting; ignition point, contact, both licensed deployments spent per bout at documented rates. Shadow-adjacent — source protected, here as everywhere; sixteen months; deployment: none, ever, anywhere public; the cost continuous and the count per week still falling; useful nowhere yet, third year of that answer begins.*
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-17.md:81:And then the arithmetic was read into the record in the tournament's own voice — each result given as the tournament gave everything, name and academy and fact — and the delegation stood on the Concourse floor and heard the math the year had built. One continental title, Copper: Brom of Halcenvane. One Iron semifinalist: Lira of Halcenvane, and the hall's sound at her name said the broadside arithmetic had been read as widely as it had been printed. One Iron quarterfinal win of record over Ternhall's first seed: Karis of Halcenvane. One round of sixteen: Ephram of Halcenvane. Team trial: third place, over Rhagen Institute, after a semifinal against the eventual champions that the official summary itself — the adjudication office's dry prose achieving, for one clause, something like feeling — described as *contested at championship quality through two exchanges.* Accumulated delegation standing: third of fourteen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:25:- **Seventeen: the rest day** (BOOK_MAP §6; recommended the eleventh of Sowing). The walk through the city at rest: the hiring board, the ranked back room, the queue outside a shut registry annex, the woodcut with the eyes cut too old, four bars of a quick-march. **The blue door:** Lira's annexation; Brom's food and the cake he paid for three days early; Karis brings nothing and writes nothing; **Seln comes to the table and sits down at it** (Seln cutaway ≈500 beforehand, deciding nothing aloud; no reason stated). The table's registers; Karis's first notebook page; the proprietor's account.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:27:- **The new volume's first entry:** the inventory at seventeen, with six fragments and their notes, "Still open. Still real. Patience." Use "since last spring" for the Shadow fragment's age (C9).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:42:- Recall sixteen without the source's "wall above a river, a delegation four days gone" (C7).
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-12.md:97:Which put the delegation's Iron campaign to bed with its final arithmetic: Lira a continental semifinalist, Karis a quarterfinal winner over Ternhall's best, Ephram's round-of-sixteen price of admission paid in full — and both Halcenvane semifinal exits administered by Auremont, whose Iron entries now held one line of the bracket's final and whose whole program had spent the fortnight demonstrating what the deep blue and silver actually meant: not a school. A *watershed*. Every current in the tournament eventually ran through them. "Keep the arithmetic where the trial briefing can see it," was Rooke's note on the standings sheet. "We've now lost to their third-best and their fourth-best, honorably, by margins. The team trial seeds us against the whole set at once."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-009.md:13:Finals day comes up off the harbor. The bank was spent last night by minute. Karis's half-beat lives only behind his eyes. Shadow is sealed. The ring is built to Auremont's specification. The Compact row is full for the first time, and at its end, in the chair that waited, a man in plain greys has been in place for an hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/packets/MOVEMENT-009.md:42:- **Shadow is deployed nowhere,** including in the fourth exchange's extremity.
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-02.md:125:"Cycle runs three years." The first sheet was the continent, and Cael, who had crossed a fingernail's width of it in sixteen years, spent the briefing's opening minute recalibrating the scale of his own biography. "Every chartered academy may enter the qualifying season. Four regional meets for us, autumn through midwinter — here, here, Fenmark's region here, and the fourth back this side of the hills. Standings accumulate across all four. Cross the continental threshold and you go to the finals in the spring." His hand moved north and east, to a dot at a crossing of four drawn roads and a river. "Norhold. Trade city, four times Ostrand. This cycle's host. Fourteen delegations, maybe fifteen, from a continent of two hundred-odd chartered academies. Understand the arithmetic: qualifying is the mountain. The finals are the view."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-16.md:15:And once, on the first afternoon, the category nearly filled. A Silver Rank 5 from one of the eastern academies, eliminated in the round of sixteen and with nothing left to lose but the number beside his name, came to the register with his form filled in, stood at the board for some minutes reading the two posted ratings and the blank line beneath them, and put the form back inside his coat, and went away. The steward, who saw it, entered nothing, because a form not filed is not a document. Half the court saw it too. By evening it had reached the delegation's table as a story, and it was Ephram who supplied the verdict the found family kept: "He did the arithmetic in public. That's the whole category's problem, standing at a board in a wet coat."
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-01.md:19:*Inventory at year's open, for the record, because the year is going to ask: six confirmed fragments. Wind-adjacent, Pressure-adjacent, Iron-adjacent, Compression-adjacent, Ember-adjacent, Shadow-adjacent. One anomaly, session nine, unreproduced, uncounted. One semester stamp, formally renewed, filed at a records hall on this bluff and mirrored at a registry I have never seen.*
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-01.md:21:*I am sixteen years old and I am enrolled at a chartered academy that intends to be measured against the whole continent this year, and the honest last line of the recess is this: for the first time since Denvash, I do not know what my part in the season is. Everyone else's part has a bracket. Open the year and find out.*
books/book-05-the-silver-standard/chapters/chapter-01.md:131:Seln processed the queue at the speed of a man who had never once been asked to hurry and had never once needed to be — a Bronze-tier assessment clerk, Shadow Path per the registry's plain entry, which was the whole of what the registry knew and precisely as much as it was ever going to. When Cael reached the counter he took the form, read it entirely — he read every form entirely, from everyone, which was the camouflage; a man who reads everything is remembered for reading nothing in particular — and stamped the received line.
